**Observations**: Read lengths varied from 150-200 bp, with a median fragment length of 179 bp. 41.17477 % of OTUs had no match. Only one OTU is associated with *Salamandra salamandra* with a cumulative 23 reads across all samples, but none in the negatives. Only one OTU was associated with humans and was abundant in one particular sample, CID.P.13. However, no human OTUs were observed in the negatives. There were 96 OTUs observed in the negatives. These were removed from the samples. Certain pathogenic taxa such as fungal, *Pythium*, and Nematoda, *Rhabditidae*, were also observed but were not ubiquitous among the samples. The vast majority of reads, however, are associated with the target prey taxa. Average % similarity among retained OTUs was 97.2 ± 3.3 %, with a final 218 OTUs retained.

Regarding taxonomic resolution, although several OTUs were assigned to Arachnida, these do not have high resolution beyond Family, and may reflect a limitation of the reference database for these taxa. There was also a lack of consistent top matches for taxa in the Order Diptera, such that it was often more prudent to assign them as generic Diptera.

Some OTUs require further inspection, Vanessa recommends the following: \>98.5% for species; \>97% for genus; \>95% family; \>90% order; \>85% class; \>80 phylum; \<80% no match. The following are a list of changes made based on individual inspection.

