### Replicates

During the extraction process several samples were extracted twice. These serve as extraction replicates. Ideally, these samples will have the same prey results. For this I isolated just the replicate samples, designate a new variable as the sample name (to groups replicates from the same source), then generate an occurrence matrix. For each prey group, is replicates match then the mean should equal 1 (e.g. 1~Rep1~ + 1~Rep2~ / 2~No.Replicates~ = 1); while a mean of 0.5 would suggest a mismatch (e.g. 1~Rep1~ + 0~Rep2~ / 2~No.Replicates~ = 0.5). For each replicate pair the average across all prey groups identified is used as an indication of concordance. Ideally, these samples will have the same prey results. Using an occurrence matrix (1 = present; 0 = absent) for each prey group among replicate pairs the mean was calculated and averaged across all prey groups. A score closer to 1 indicates perfect match compared to a score of 0.5 indicating complete mismatch. To determine the impact of potentially confounding variables, such as weather, sample date, extraction date, PCR enrichment date we tested for correlation between variables.

```{r Compare Replicates, warning=FALSE, error=FALSE}
## Generate lists to save results 
incidence_rep_raw <- list() # list of samples with prey occurrence/absence

# Restrict the data to just replicates
merged.table.prey[merged.table.prey$type=="replicate",] -> replicate.table

# Create new variable that is just the sample name
substr(replicate.table[replicate.table$type=="replicate",]$sample,1,8) ->
  replicate.table$rep_sample

# Convert into incidence/frequency matrix
levels(as.factor(replicate.table$rep_sample)) ->
  replicate.samples

## First we want to detect species presence (0,1) in each sample and by group
for ( i in seq_along(replicate.samples)){
  # Index of variable
  incidence_rep_raw[[i]] <- replicate.table %>%
    # Apply filters
    filter(diet.status == "ok" & 
            replicate.table[,"rep_sample"]==replicate.samples[i]
             ) %>%
    # Select the level of prey
    select(prey.id, sample, blankfiltered.reads) %>%
    #summarise(reads = sum(blankfiltered.reads)) %>%
    group_by(prey.id, sample) %>%
    unique.data.frame() %>%
    mutate_if(is.numeric, ~1 * (. > 0)) %>%
    pivot_wider(names_from = sample, values_from = blankfiltered.reads, values_fn = mean) %>%
    mutate_if(is.numeric, replace_na, 0) %>%
    as.data.frame()
}

## Set species names are st as row names
for (i in seq_along(replicate.samples)){
  row.names(incidence_rep_raw[[i]]) <- incidence_rep_raw[[i]]$prey.id
  incidence_rep_raw[[i]]$prey.id <- NULL
}

## create incidence_raw
names(incidence_rep_raw) <- replicate.samples

## create incidence_frequency object
incidence_rep_freq <- lapply(incidence_rep_raw, as.incfreq)

# Print the mean across replicates.
# The closer to 1 the more consistent, the closer to 0.5 the less consistent
for (i in seq_along(replicate.samples)){
  if (length(colnames(incidence_rep_raw[[i]])) >= 2){
    print(noquote(
      paste(replicate.samples[[i]], " = ",
                signif(mean(apply(incidence_rep_raw[[i]],1,mean)),3)
      )))
}}
```

In only 1 of the replicates was there identical prey detection. In 2 replicates, the prey detected were completely different, and in the remaining 2 it was consistent half of the time. This suggests that the results we observe per individual probably only indicate a proportion of what we might find. This is considered expected in a metabarcoding study. While fecal pellets were masticated, they were not exposed to a lengthier metallic bead homogenization that would better mix samples at the cost of additional degradation and added expense.

