## Cumulative Prey

### Prey Richness

Estimating species richness alone entail the following limitations, (i) that observed species richness is sensitive to sample size and rare species are often missed, and (ii) species richness doesn't account for relative abundance of species in an assemblage [@chao2014ecomono]. To address the former (i), standardized comparisons of species richness can be made after interpolation with rarefaction. Hill numbers are a diversity indices that incorporate relative abundance and species richness to estimating taxonomic diversity in a manner that allows for comparisons between multiple assemblages.

For each variable considered (i.e. site, habitat, extraction block, etc.) rarefaction curves are estimated to see if there is a difference in species estimates between variables that can be attributed to sample coverage. In order to run some steps in this analysis we were required to remove individuals from analysis with fewer than 100 total dietary reads, as well as those sites with only single samples.

iNext reportedly comes into errors with incidence matrices of 2-4 columns (sites) and a total richness (rows) of up to 15. Additionally, because ggiNEXT can only handle up to 8 different groups I will need to limit the visuals to only the eight most well sampled groups.

With the richness results I generated a [GLM](https://sscc.wisc.edu/sscc/pubs/glm-r/), transforming response variables to be done by least squares. I've used the "poisson" family to use the "log" link function. Richness in this instance is the cumulative number of species observed in a group. While the rural habitat has a greater species richness (S.obs) it does have a lower sample coverage estimate (SC) and ultimately was not found to be significant.


