## Commentary

The results of the GLM suggest very little difference in the prey richness between groups. No difference was detected between sample sites, extraction block, enrichment block, or habitats. One significant difference was observed between some sample dates, with a slight but probably negligible decrease in observed prey richness over time (*r* = -0.18).

The PermANOVA suggests a significant difference in prey variance between each condition, with perhaps the exception of extraction date. The first three eigenvalues of each group only describe 10% of total variation. Between habitats, we see a significant difference in prey composition, that could feasibly be structured into 4 different clusters. However, it isn't clear when taken as a whole which of the prey variables or conditions is driving this pattern.

**Results**, The results differ pretty markedly when performing the analysis with a Urban/Rural contrast versus Urban/Urban Park/Rural comparison. In the three habitat scenario *Armadillidium* is more common in urban habitats than parks or rural habitats, while *Lumbricus* is significantly more common in parks, mostly because of the contribution from Parque Cidade do Porto. However, when performing the analysis with only two habitats, the pattern is lost, this despite the observation that the general patterns still persist, but are no longer identified as statistically significant. What we can see instead from a simple PCA on prey presence is that *Armadillidium* and *Lumbricus* are the two genera that drive the majority of differences between Urban/Rural variance.

**Repeat Sampling**

Still to consider is the impact that having re-sampled locations have on the data. For this I perform the analysis for each location from one sample date, the one which has the largest sample size. The results was much lower sample size, particularly for urban parks. Any possible significance between habitat type richness (*p* \< 0.1) was lost. The bias in sample date and habitat type persisted, however, instead of urban parks being sampled later in the season than others, it was now sampled earlier than other habitats. The PCA of PermANOVA results suggests a similar pattern with less described variability (8% variation described by first 2 PCs) but with a bit more definition between habitat types. Similarity percentages between habitats didn't dramatically increase with perhaps fewer prey taxa identified as significantly different between urban and ex-urban habitats. Overall, it doesn't appear that by excluding the data to only single sample dates has much of an impact on the results.

**Reduced taxonomic resolution**

Following the initial analysis, some results were seen to be potentially uninformative or misleading as a result of the hierarchical taxonomic identification. As an example, between Urban Park and Urban habitats, a significant difference was observed in the presence of Isopoda and Armadillidium. The latter is a Family within the former, and the lack of resolution may result from either a limitation of the reference database.

Reducing the overall taxonomic resolution of the data to the Family I wanted to see if the results would change. At the Family level, it appears that the results remain more or less consistent, however, fewer prey or delineated as significantly different, as one might expect with a coarser resolution and therefore fewer prey categories. Prey richness is reduced overall, but variation between habitat types, sample site, and sample date remain significant, if not more clear visually. However, this doesn't necessarily resolve the aforementioned example where the issue lies at the Family level distinction. Performing the same analysis using the Order level as the finest resolution appears to suppress nearly all variance between groups.

**Prey Diversity by Phylum**

Taking a closer look, I want to within each group (habitat, sample date, site) has the most different types of prey for each of the different prey Phylum. To do this I repeated the PermANOVA analysis using only one Phylum at a time, removing samples for which no prey of that given Phylum were detected. Comparing habitats, the results suggest that Annelida are not significantly different between habitat types; Mollusca, do appear to be differ significantly between habitat types; and Arthropoda differ significantly between habitat types, in particular between urban parks and urban habitats.

Performing the same analysis with sites, it appears that the difference in Arthropoda between habitats is driven largely by Parque Cidade Oriental (Sul), which has a greater occurrence of Araneae, Cecidomyiidae, and generic Isopoda than do other sites. It would seem that the majority of the variance between urban parks and other habitats would be explained by this specific site, at least as far as Arhtropoda are concerned. It also appears that the differences in Mollusca between habitats is driven largely by three sites, Mindelo (MIN,ex-urban), Sr. Matasinhos (MAT, urban), and Parque Cidade (CID, urban park) which make up the majority of mollusca observation in each of their respective habitats. MIN has a 8 taxa groups identified in roughly equal frequency, while Milacidae are the most common mollusca in MAT and CID, followed up by primarily snails (Chondrinidae, Cochlicopidae, Helicidae) in the former and slugs (Agriolimacidae,Testacellidae) in the latter.


A significant variance between habitats was observed in Arthropoda and Mollusca, but not so with Annelida. However, Annelida prey richness was significantly higher in ex-urban habitats that in urban habitats.


**Observation** When comparing between habitat one phylum at a type, we see that among Annelidae, rural populations appear to have a significantly more diverse diet than their urban counterparts, which in turn appear to be consuming primarily *Lumbricus*; this appears to be especially true in Parque Cidade. Among Mollusca, while some prey taxa, Arion and Oxychilus, appear to be more abundant in rural habitats, this may on account that they were only or more often observed in rural populations. No differences in Arthropods were observed.
